<DocsLayout
  title="Server API"
  subtitle="How the nikcli Hono server exposes CLI, TUI, mobile, workspace, and generated Effect HttpApi routes."
  description="Reference for nikcli’s Hono server routes, SDK boundary, mobile auth, and generated Effect HttpApi routes."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="overview" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The server in <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/server.ts</code> is the shared HTTP surface for
      web clients, the desktop app, the TUI control channel, mobile pairing, PTYs, workspace routing,
      connector settings, MCP, analytics, and generated SDK-compatible APIs.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is built on Hono. Most route groups are classic Hono route files under
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/routes</code>. A second set of typed route groups lives under
      <code>packages/nikcli/src/server/httpapi</code> and is activated by
      <code>NIKCLI_EXPERIMENTAL_HTTPAPI</code> through <code>HttpApiBridge</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="doc-grid not-prose">
      <DocCard title="Classic Hono routes" eyebrow="Default">
        <p><code>/session</code>, <code>/mobile</code>, <code>/provider</code>, <code>/mcp</code>, <code>/connectors</code>, <code>/analytics</code>, and other route groups mounted directly in <code>server.ts</code>.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Effect HttpApi routes" eyebrow="Typed">
        <p>Typed groups for SDK generation and stricter request/response contracts. The bridge handles matching paths before the classic route group runs.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Workspace-aware execution" eyebrow="Context">
        <p>Every non-user request gets an instance context from <code>x-nikcli-directory</code>, <code>directory</code>, <code>x-nikcli-workspace</code>, or the route session.</p>
      </DocCard>
      <DocCard title="Mobile bearer auth" eyebrow="Remote">
        <p>Mobile tokens and user session tokens can satisfy server auth before Basic Auth is checked.</p>
      </DocCard>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="start" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Start a server</h2>
    <p>
      Use <code>nikcli serve</code> for the general API server, <code>nikcli web</code> for the
      web app workflow, or <code>nikcli mobile serve</code> for the mobile-first host. The TUI also
      starts an internal worker server when network options are provided.
    </p>
    <DocCode language="bash">
nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4096

NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD=secret \
  nikcli serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096

NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD=secret \
  nikcli mobile serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096 --pair
    </DocCode>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Command</th><th>Primary routes</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Local API</td><td><code>nikcli serve</code></td><td>All mounted server routes</td><td>Safe for loopback development. Password is optional on loopback.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Public API</td><td><code>nikcli serve --hostname 0.0.0.0</code></td><td>All mounted server routes</td><td>Set <code>NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD</code> or use loopback Tailscale identity mode.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile host</td><td><code>nikcli mobile serve --pair</code></td><td><code>/mobile/*</code>, <code>/user/*</code>, shared project/session routes</td><td>Bearer token auth is minted by the mobile pairing commands.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>TUI external</td><td><code>nikcli --port 0</code></td><td>TUI worker server plus standard API</td><td>Used when the terminal UI needs network-visible access.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="pipeline" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Request pipeline</h2>
    <p>The mounted app in <code>Server.App</code> processes requests in this order:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Global error mapping converts known domain errors into JSON status responses.</li>
      <li>Public share routes run before user/server auth: <code>/s/:shareID</code>, <code>/share/:shareID</code>, and <code>/api/share/:shareID</code>.</li>
      <li>User auth middleware attaches an account session when the request carries a valid user token.</li>
      <li>Server-level auth checks mobile bearer tokens, Tailscale identity headers, and optional Basic Auth.</li>
      <li>CORS allows local, app, Tailscale, <code>nikcli.store</code>, and configured origins.</li>
      <li><code>/global</code> health and global routes mount before workspace scoping.</li>
      <li>Workspace context resolves directory, workspace, and remote workspace proxying.</li>
      <li><code>/doc</code> serves OpenAPI documentation generated from the Hono app.</li>
      <li>The Effect <code>HttpApiBridge</code> handles supported paths when <code>NIKCLI_EXPERIMENTAL_HTTPAPI</code> is enabled.</li>
      <li>Classic Hono route groups are mounted after the bridge.</li>
    </ol>
    <DocCallout type="info" title="Bridge before classic routes">
      <p>
        The bridge check runs after query validation and workspace context, but before the classic
        route groups. This lets typed handlers own a route when the feature flag is active while the
        old Hono route remains the fallback.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="auth" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Auth layers</h2>
    <p>
      Server auth is layered so browser user sessions, mobile device tokens, Tailscale identity, and
      Basic Auth can coexist.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Accepted input</th><th>Where checked</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>User endpoints</td><td><code>/user/login</code>, <code>/user/register</code>, <code>/user/status</code></td><td><code>server.ts</code> auth bypass</td><td>Public by design so accounts can be created and login status can be checked.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>User session</td><td>Bearer token from user auth</td><td><code>userAuthMiddleware</code></td><td>Sets <code>userSession</code>. If present with a bearer token, request proceeds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile token</td><td><code>Authorization: Bearer</code> or <code>?token=</code></td><td><code>MobileAuth.verify</code></td><td>Sets <code>mobileAuth</code> for mobile route handlers and websocket-style token flows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tailscale identity</td><td><code>Tailscale-User-Login</code></td><td>Loopback listener with <code>NIKCLI_SERVER_TAILSCALE_AUTH</code></td><td>Allows configured users from <code>NIKCLI_SERVER_TAILSCALE_USERS</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Basic Auth</td><td>Username and password</td><td><code>basicAuth</code></td><td>Username defaults to <code>nikcli</code>; password comes from <code>NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD</code>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="bash">
NIKCLI_SERVER_USERNAME=nikcli \
NIKCLI_SERVER_PASSWORD=secret \
  nikcli serve --hostname 0.0.0.0 --port 4096

NIKCLI_SERVER_TAILSCALE_AUTH=1 \
NIKCLI_SERVER_TAILSCALE_USERS=alice@example.com,bob@example.com \
  nikcli serve --hostname 127.0.0.1 --port 4096
    </DocCode>
  </section>

  <section id="workspace-context" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Workspace context</h2>
    <p>
      After auth, non-user routes run inside an instance context. The server chooses the directory
      from <code>directory</code> query, <code>x-nikcli-directory</code> header, current process cwd,
      or the directory stored on a session referenced by the route path.
    </p>
    <p>
      Workspace routing then looks at <code>workspace</code>, <code>x-nikcli-workspace</code>, or
      the route session's <code>workspaceID</code>. Local workspaces replace the directory with the
      workspace target directory. Remote workspaces are proxied through <code>ServerProxy</code> for
      both HTTP and websocket upgrades.
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Priority</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>directory</code> query</td><td>High</td><td>Sets the instance directory unless a workspace target overrides it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>x-nikcli-directory</code></td><td>High</td><td>Same as query, used by SDK and mobile clients.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session route id</td><td>Fallback</td><td>If the path starts with <code>/session/:id</code>, the server can recover the session's directory and workspace.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>x-nikcli-workspace</code></td><td>Workspace override</td><td>Routes to local or remote workspace target.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="routes" class="doc-section">
